mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 have "done_involuntarily" and "hero_sacrifices"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 has "done_involuntarily" and "done_involuntarily → without_happiness" and "hero_sacrifices"; they differ when only "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "the hero or martyr voluntarily does without happiness for something he prizes more than his own happiness" are true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "the hero or martyr voluntarily does without happiness for something he prizes more than his own happiness" are true and "people do without happiness" is false. Is S2, "Unquestionably it is possible to do without happiness; it is done involuntarily by nineteen-twentieths of mankind, even in those parts of our present world which are least deep in barbarism; and it often has to be done voluntarily by the hero or the martyr, for the sake of something which he prizes more than his individual happiness.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 has "sacrifice_made → believes_benefits_others"; they differ when only "the hero or martyr would make the sacrifice" is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the hero or martyr would make the sacrifice" is true and "the hero or martyr believes the sacrifice earns for others immunity from similar sacrifices, rather than only making their lot like his" is false. Is S5, "Would it be made, if he thought that his renunciation of happiness for himself would produce no fruit for any of his fellow creatures, but to make their lot like his, and place them also in the condition of persons who have renounced happiness?", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 has "◇imitate"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "can" or "should" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "possibly, men follow the example of the one who renounces" is false. Is S7, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 have "¬admirable → ¬O imitate"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 has "done_involuntarily → without_happiness" and "¬admirable → ¬O imitate"; they differ when only "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "the one who renounces is deserving of honour and admiration" are true, which the first allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily, then people do without happiness"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1 does not [in 1 of 2 runs]
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily, then people do without happiness"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
